---
title: "Language overview"
description: "Learn QQL scripts, statements, values, vector roles, and clause ordering."
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

QQL keywords are ASCII case-insensitive. Identifiers preserve source spelling.
Statements in a script are separated by semicolons; a trailing semicolon is
allowed.

## Statement families

| Family | Statements |
|---|---|
| Retrieval | `QUERY`, `SCROLL`, `COUNT` |
| Data | `UPSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `CLEAR PAYLOAD`, `DELETE PAYLOAD`, `DELETE VECTOR` |
| Schema | `CREATE`, `ALTER`, `DROP`, `SHOW` |

{% qqlExample title="A mixed script" %}
CREATE COLLECTION notes (
  dense VECTOR(384, COSINE)
);

UPSERT INTO notes VALUES {id: 1, text: 'first note'};

QUERY TEXT 'note' FROM notes USING dense LIMIT 5;
{% /qqlExample %}

## Values

QQL supports strings, signed integers, floats, booleans, `null`, ordered
objects, and lists. Point IDs are unsigned integers or strings. Dense, sparse,
and multidense vectors have distinct shapes:

| Kind | Shape |
|---|---|
| Dense | `[0.1, 0.2, 0.3]` |
| Sparse | `{indices: [1, 9], values: [0.4, 0.7]}` |
| Multidense | `[[0.1, 0.2], [0.3, 0.4]]` |

## Clause order

Query clauses are unique and ordered:

`USING` → `PREFETCH` → `WHERE` → `SHARD` → `PARAMS` → `SCORE THRESHOLD`
→ `GROUP BY` → payload/vector selectors → `LIMIT` → `OFFSET`.

The parser rejects repeated and out-of-order clauses rather than silently
accepting a prefix.
